{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1890 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "cf5ed945782223b090783bb099eced78012cebe92ed2e8c04241fbf7a9b4b0d6" :edb/db-before-t 1889 :edb/db-after-t 1890 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535202 50 #inst "2026-10-08T19:25:25.697Z" 13194139535202 true] [17592186179527 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}) \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}$, where $\\mathbf F(x, y) = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction." 13194139535202 false] [17592186179527 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}) \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $\\mathbf F(x, y) = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction." 13194139535202 true] [17592186179527 1145 "The notation $\\displaystyle\\oint$ is used to remind us that we're dealing with a line integral over a *closed* curve.\n\nWe will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad 0 \\leq t \\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C$, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos{t}, \\qquad y = \\sin{t}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) &= yi + xj = \\sin ti + \\cos tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-\\sin ti + \\cos tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t) &= (\\sin ti + \\cos tj) \\cdot (-\\sin ti + \\cos tj) \\\\\n&=-\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t \\\\\n&= - (1 - \\cos^{2} t) + \\cos^{2} t \\\\\n&= 2\\cos^{2} t - 1 \\\\\n&= \\cos 2t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)F(r) \\cdot dr &= ∫_{0}^{2π}F(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}\\cos 2tdt \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{2}\\sin 2t]_{0}^{2π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\sin 4π - \\frac{1}{2}\\sin (0) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535202 false] [17592186179527 1145 "The notation $\\displaystyle\\oint$ is used to remind us that we're dealing with a line integral over a *closed* curve.\n\nWe will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n\\qquad 0 \\leq t \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos{t}, \\qquad\ny = \\sin{t}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,j=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t)+{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535202 true] [17592186179528 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}$, where $\\mathbf F(x, y) = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the curve $y^3 = x-5$ traversed from the point $A(5,0)$ to the point $B(6,1)$." 13194139535202 false] [17592186179528 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $\\mathbf F(x, y) = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the curve $y^3 = x-5$ traversed from the point $A(5,0)$ to the point $B(6,1).$" 13194139535202 true] [17592186179528 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Making $x$ the subject of the equation, we have\n\n$$\ny^3 = x-5 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = y^3 + 5\n$$\n\nSo, we can parametrize the curve using\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= (t^{3} + 5)i + tj, 0 \\le t \\le 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C$, we have\n\n$$\nx = t^3+5, \\qquad y = t\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) &= yi + xj \\\\\n&= ti + (t^{3} + 5)j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{3} + 5)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t)j \\\\\n&= 3t^{2}i + j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t) &= (ti + (t^{3} + 5)j) \\cdot (3t^{2}i + j) \\\\\n&= t \\cdot 3t^{2} + (t^{3} + 5) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 3t^{3} + t^{3} + 5 \\\\\n&= 4t^{3} + 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)F \\cdot dr &= ∫_{0}^{1}F(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}4t^{3} + 5dt \\\\\n&= [t^{4} + 5t]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 1 + 5 - 0 \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535202 false] [17592186179528 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Making $x$ the subject of the equation, we have\n\n$$\ny^3 = x-5\n\\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad\nx = y^3 + 5.\n$$\n\nSo, we can parametrize the curve using\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =({t}^{3}+5)\\,i+t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = t^3+5, \\qquad\ny = t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,j \\\\  & =t\\,i+({t}^{3}+5)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+5)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+({t}^{3}+5)\\,j)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =t\\cdot 3{t}^{2}+({t}^{3}+5)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3{t}^{3}+{t}^{3}+5 \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{t}^{3}+5\\,dt \\\\  & ={[{t}^{4}+5t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+5-0 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535202 true] [17592186179529 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}$, where $\\mathbf F(x, y) =(1-y)^2\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(0, 2)$ to the point $B(1,0)$." 13194139535202 false] [17592186179529 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $\\mathbf F(x, y) =(1-y)^2\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(0, 2)$ to the point $B(1,0).$" 13194139535202 true] [17592186179529 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = 2\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= a + t(b - a) \\\\\n&= 2j + t[i - 2j] \\\\\n&= ti + (2 - 2t)j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t = 1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C$, we have\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad y = 2-2t\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) &= (1 - y)^{2}i + xj \\\\\n&= (1 - (2 - 2t))^{2}i + tj \\\\\n&= (2t - 1)^{2}i + tj \\\\\n&= (4t^{2} - 4t + 1)i + tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(2 - 2t)j \\\\\n&= i - 2j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t) &= ((4t^{2} - 4t + 1)i + tj) \\cdot (i - 2j) \\\\\n&= (4t^{2} - 4t + 1) \\cdot (1) + (t) \\cdot (-2) \\\\\n&= 4t^{2} - 6t + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)F \\cdot dr &= ∫_{0}^{1}F(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}4t^{2} - 6t + 1dt \\\\\n&= (\\frac{4}{3}t^{3} - 3t^{2} + t) \\mid _{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{4}{3} - 3 + 1) - 0 \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535202 false] [17592186179529 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are \n$\\mathbf{a} = 2\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{i}.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =2j+t[i-2j] \\\\  & =t\\,i+(2-2t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t = 1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad\n\ty = 2-2t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(1-y)^{2}\\,i+x\\,j \\\\  & =(1-(2-2t))^{2}\\,i+t\\,j \\\\  & =(2t-1)^{2}\\,i+t\\,j \\\\  & =(4{t}^{2}-4t+1)\\,i+t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-2t)j \\\\  & =i-2j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((4{t}^{2}-4t+1)\\,i+t\\,j)\\cdot (i-2j) \\\\  & =(4{t}^{2}-4t+1)\\cdot (1)+(t)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}-6t+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{t}^{2}-6t+1\\,dt \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{3}-3+1)-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535202 true] [17592186179665 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337273 13194139535202 true] [17592186179665 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337280 13194139535202 true] [17592186179665 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337287 13194139535202 true] [17592186179665 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337294 13194139535202 true] [17592186179665 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337301 13194139535202 true] [17592186179666 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337224 13194139535202 true] [17592186179666 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337231 13194139535202