\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3-2t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+3t)j \\\\  & =-2\\,i+3\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =({e}^{3-2t}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+3t)\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\,i+3\\,j) \\\\  & ={e}^{3-2t}\\cdot (-2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+3t)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-2{e}^{3-2t}+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-2{e}^{3-2t}+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+3t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535202 true] [17592186179942 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535202 true] [17592186179942 1100 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(1,-1)$ to the point $B(-3,1).$" 13194139535202 true] [17592186179942 1100 "Evaluate $∫_(C)F \\cdot dr$, where $F(x, y) = (x + y)i + π\\cos πyj$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(1,-1)$ to the point $B(-3, 1)$." 13194139535202 false] [17592186179942 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i-j$ and $b=-3\\,i+j.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =(i-j)+t[-3\\,i+j-(i-j)] \\\\  & =(i-j)+t(-4\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =(1-4t)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t=1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=1-4t,\\,y=2t-1.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(x+y)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y\\,j \\\\  & =(1-4t+(2t-1))\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡((2t-1)\\pi )\\,j \\\\  & =-2t\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡((2t-1)\\pi )\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-4t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)j \\\\  & =-4\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(-2t\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡((2t-1)\\pi )\\,j)\\cdot (-4\\,i+2j) \\\\  & =-2t\\cdot (-4)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡((2t-1)\\pi )\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8t+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡((2t-1)\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}8t+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡((2t-1)\\pi )\\,dt \\\\  & ={4{t}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡((2t-1)\\pi )|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(4+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )-(0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =(4+0)-(0+0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535202 true] [17592186179947 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535202 true] [17592186179947 1100 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $y=2{sin}^{2}⁡x$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to the point $B(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2).$" 13194139535202 true] [17592186179947 1100 "Evaluate $∫_(C)F \\cdot dr$, where $F(x, y) = \\cos xi + yj$ and $C$ is the curve $y = 2\\sin^{2} x$ traversed from the point $A(0, 0)$ to the point $B(\\frac{π}{2}, 2)$." 13194139535202 false] [17592186179947 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Since $y$ is the subject of the equation, a parametrization is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =t\\,i+2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=2{sin}^{2}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{sin}^{2}⁡t)j \\\\  & =i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\text{ })\\cdot (i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2{sin}^{4}⁡t]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535202 true] [17592186337224 1017 #edb/ref 17592186337225 13194139535202 true] [17592186337224 1089 "q-124706" 13194139535202 true] [17592186337224 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535202 true] [17592186337224 1100 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve ${y}^{3}=x+3$ traversed from the point $A(-3,0)$ to the point $B(5,2).$" 13194139535202 true] [17592186337224 1113 #uuid "cb93a673-1648-507b-9701-d342ceacf5ab" 13194139535202 true] [17592186337224 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Making $x$ the subject of the equation, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}=x+3\\,⟹\\,x={y}^{3}-3.\n$$\n\nSo, the parametrization of the curve is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =({t}^{3}-3)\\,i+t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-3,\\,y=t